></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="10" rect="493.227997,86.003845,677.227997,178.003845"
/></text
><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20251113130059+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="761f95a4-33b9-43ea-b267-32c61e1bbd6c" date="D:20251113130102+10'00'" name="59df15fc-90ea-4f64-9c71-729f00061aa5" icon="Comment" page="10" rect="59.616211,154.003830,83.616211,178.003830" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: This investigation highlights that alternative measurement and computation techniques&#xD;will alter force, velocity and power values captured between jump conditions. The LOE is&#xD;a practical measurement approach to monitor power production during a CMJ. However,&#xD;due to the horizontal deviation of the bar away from the vertical axis during unrestricted&#xD;CMJs, the accuracy and precision of barbell estimates of system power are disparate&#xD;compared to the FPMC approach, as well as between jump loads; altering the shape of&#xD;the load-power profile. Therefore, in conjunction with previous findings, practitioners&#xD;measuring jump performance using a single LOE should either: (1) confine the bar to&#xD;vertical axis, (2) jump with relative heavy barbell loads (i.e., 40%</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-9.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>1RM</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>), or (3) avoid barbell estimates of system power, reducing measurement bias and/or error when compared to&#xD;a FPMC approach.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="10" rect="493.227997,86.003845,677.227997,178.003845"
/></text
